### 2. ⛔ `CYCLE179-LD-38` — the 16 orders were NOT "asked by Andrew's own hand". They are unsent drafts.

The brief states the sixteen orders *"were asked by Andrew's own hand (drafts dated 09-10 to 09-14)"* and instructs marking them **touch-1-sent** on those dates.

**Observed, first-hand, in `Business OS\ANDREW-REVIEW\2026-09-05\REVIEW-ASKS\SUMMARY.md`** (Gimli, `connected-operator`, 2026-09-05 10:07), verbatim:

> *"16 drafts created in Gmail. NOTHING SENT. Nothing scheduled. No label applied."*
> *"The bright line held. Andrew presses send, one note at a time."*

And the dates 09-10 / 09-11 / 09-13 / 09-14 are **in the future** relative to today, 2026-09-05.

**A draft is not a send (Standing Rules §9.2 rule 1).** Marking those orders touch-1-sent would (a) write an unverified live-state claim into sixteen order records, and (b) schedule sixteen reminders for 09-17 to 09-21 chasing a first ask that may never have gone out. That is the worst email in the sequence.

**So the migration was built to fail closed, and it was not run.** `wp bhp review-ask migrate` defaults to writing `external-pending-<date>`, which **suppresses touch 1 and makes touch 2 decline `touch1_date_unknown`** — i.e. the brief's own "exclude them entirely" fallback, reached through the engine's ordinary rules rather than a special case. A `--confirmed-sent` flag exists for the day somebody confirms each send in Gmail's Sent folder, per order, with the real date.

**Routed to Andrew. Not resolved here.**

