### The day math, as implemented

```
VISIT LANE   anchor = visit date from `bhp_school_visits`, at LOCAL midnight
             + 7  days when the order holds ONE chapter book
             + 10 days when it holds TWO OR MORE
WEB LANE     anchor = order completion (existing fallback chain)
             + 10 days                     ** PENDING ANDREW **
TOUCH 2      + 7 days after touch 1 ACTUALLY went out, both lanes
SEND WINDOW  08:00 to 11:59 site-local, enforced as a decline
```

**The engine's arithmetic reproduces Gimli's hand-built schedule exactly**, and the new suite asserts that as a table:

| Visit | Books | Engine says | Gimli's drafts say |
|---|---|---|---|
| Dallas Harris 2026-09-03 | 1 | 2026-09-10 | 2026-09-10 (5 drafts) |
| Dallas Harris 2026-09-03 | 2+ | 2026-09-13 | 2026-09-13 (3 drafts) |
| Liberty 2026-09-04 | 1 | 2026-09-11 | 2026-09-11 (5 drafts) |
| Liberty 2026-09-04 | 2+ | 2026-09-14 | 2026-09-14 (3 drafts) |

⚠ **Computed, not observed.** The engine side is arithmetic read off the source; the Gimli side is read from his file. Neither was executed.

### Decisions I made, and why

**Chapter books are counted through `bhp_book_lookup_product()`, not by name.** That function knows the paperback and hardcover product IDs of the three titles and nothing else, so **the Activity Book is excluded structurally** rather than by a string match that would break the first time it is retitled. Two copies of the same title count as **one** book: the rule is about how many different books a child has to get through.

**The visit anchor is the visit DATE at local midnight, never the completion timestamp.** The eight Adams orders all completed within fourteen seconds of each other because Andrew flips visit orders in a batch; anchoring on completion would make the send date an artefact of his afternoon. `wp_timezone()` is used, not `strtotime()`, because PHP's process timezone is UTC on this host and would land the anchor 6-7 hours early — potentially a whole calendar day early.

**The visit date is read from the registry, never parsed out of the slug.** Live slugs look like `adams-2026-08-28` and it is tempting to `substr` it. A visit rescheduled after its slug was minted would then email every parent on the wrong day with total confidence.

**A visit order whose slug the registry does not know falls back to the completion anchor.** That can only send *later*, never earlier.

**The 90-day customer cooldown now gates touch 1 only.** ⭐ This is the single most important scoping decision in the change. Touch 1 writes the customer stamp; if the cooldown also ran on touch 2, that stamp would decline the reminder seven days later **every single time**, and Andrew's approved sequence would silently be a single ask again with nothing in any log to show it. Touch 2 is bounded by its own tighter rule instead: one reminder per order, ever, killed the moment a review appears.

**An UNAPPROVED site review suppresses the reminder.** `inc/reviews.php` holds every product review for moderation **in code**, so a parent who did exactly what touch 1 asked sits at `comment_approved = 0` until Andrew opens wp-admin. Counting only approved reviews would chase that parent for a review they had already written. Spam and trash do not count.

**The placeholder copy cannot be sent, structurally.** The web touch-1 and touch-2 sets ship `approved => false`, which is a **hard per-order decline** (`copy_not_approved`). Even switching the engine on cannot put PENDING-COPY text in front of a customer.

**The `copy_delay_mismatch` interlock was adapted, not bypassed.** It was one number compared to one number, because there was one delay and one sentence (*"turned up about three weeks ago"*) true at exactly 21 days. There is no longer one delay — +7 and +10 are both correct on the same run — so a global halt would have had to pick one and would then stop the whole run because a *different* order used a different delay. It is now **per order**, returns the same slug, and the run summary still counts it by name. The visit copy declares `delay_days => [7, 10]` and the suite asserts a delay outside the declared set still declines.

**The visit copy is true at both 7 and 10 days.** Its only time claim is *"at {SchoolName} last week"*, and its only other temporal line is a question (*"If {ChildFirstName} has had a few nights with it by now"*). Both hold at either delay. That is why the array form is honest here and would not have been for the 21-day set.

**An unresolvable merge slot is a decline, not a blank.** `{SchoolName}`, `{BookTitle}` and `{ReviewLink}` are checked before send; an email reading *"Thank you for picking up  at ."* cannot be recalled. `{ParentFirstName}` and `{ChildFirstName}` have designed fallbacks and are not checked.

**No name is ever derived from the billing name.** The billing name is the parent. The suite asserts the probe orders' `Testparent` never appears where a child's name goes.

### ⭐ A defect this change would have introduced, caught and fixed

`inc/transactional-emails.php` built the inbox preheader from a map **keyed by email ID**. Since seal 965 one ID renders two different emails, so touch 1's preview text would have appeared in the inbox beside touch 2's subject line. Fixed by capturing the preheader from the live email object at `woocommerce_email_header` time — the same pattern that file already uses for the visit slug, for the same reason.

### ⚠ What I deliberately did NOT implement

**Merry's shorter multi-book variant (`CYCLE179-MKT-REVIEW-ASKS.md` §2) is not in the engine.** It turns on facts an automated send cannot know — *"the book the parent named at the table"* and *"which I did not expect and have not stopped grinning about"*, a sentiment about one specific morning. The primary template makes no such claim, so it is true for a two-book order too, and the multi-book difference Andrew ruled on is expressed where he put it: in the **timing**, not in a second set of words nobody approved for automated sending. **Flagged for Merry and Andrew, not decided here.**

**Touch-2 and web touch-1 copy is PENDING-COPY.** `CYCLE179-MKT-REVIEW-SEQ.md` did not exist at 10:36 MDT (the marketing WORKING-DRAFTS folder held only `CYCLE179-MKT-REVIEW-ASKS.md`; Merry's lock was HELD and declared it as a new file). When it lands: swap the strings **verbatim** into `bhp_review_ask_copy_web_touch1()` / `bhp_review_ask_copy_touch2()`, set `approved => true`, set `delay_days` to the delay her words are true at. Nothing else changes.

