## The backlog rule, and what the first run would send

**The header's backlog rule for the 21-day switch-on is the daily cap**, and it is unchanged: `BHP_REVIEW_ASK_DEFAULT_DAILY_CAP = 5`, **counted from the stats ledger rather than a per-run counter** (so two schedulers on one day top up to the cap instead of doubling it), with candidates sorted **longest-waiting first by the anchor**, plus the master switch defaulting **off**. Applied identically here; nothing was loosened.

⛔ **What the first run would actually send is UNVERIFIED and I will not estimate it.** It depends on the live order table, which I could not read. What can be stated structurally:

- **Web-lane orders: zero.** Every one declines `copy_not_approved` until Merry's copy lands. That is the whole backlog neutralised for the shipped build.
- **Visit-lane orders: only those past visit+7 / visit+10, capped at 5/day, oldest first.** With the 16 Dallas Harris and Liberty orders migrated as `external-pending`, they are suppressed too.
- **The engine is OFF.** `bhp_review_ask_enabled` defaults `no` and is never written by the theme.

**The command that answers this honestly, once SSH is available:**

```bash
ssh -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 -p 18765 u2488-m3vdq4niawwg@gcam1230.siteground.biz \
  "cd <staging_doc_root> && wp bhp review-ask plan \
     --dates=2026-09-05,2026-09-10,2026-09-11,2026-09-13,2026-09-14 --user=1 \
     --url=https://staging2.braveheartspublishing.com"
```

It sends nothing, writes nothing, never calls the runner and never touches the mailer, the markers, the ledger or the registries. It walks the candidates and asks the **same** `bhp_review_ask_decline_reason()` the real run uses with the clock moved to 09:00 site-local on each date, and prints per order: touch, lane, chapter-book count, visit date — then the cap and every decline reason by name.

