# R2 — 1.19.363, THE STAGING FAILURES FIXED
**Workstream `CYCLE179-LD-REVIEW-SEQ-R2` · lead-developer (Aragorn) · 2026-09-05, 11:12–11:2x MDT (machine clock, `date`, first-hand).**
Follow-on to the section above. Gandalf deployed the 1.19.362 ZIP to staging himself, ran both suites and reported the results; every failure below is **his measured output**, not my prediction.

## THE HEADLINE

**Every §1 and §4 failure in `tests/test-cycle179-review-seq.php` had ONE cause, and it was in the SUITE, not in the engine.** The fixture hooked a filter that does not exist. Nothing in the seal-965 day math, the lane split, the book counting or the cooldown scoping was wrong. Gandalf's two hypotheses — that the chapter-book count was counting the Activity Book, and that it was reading the wrong line-item field — are both **disproved by his own run**: `One chapter book counts as 1`, `Two chapter books count as 2` and `Three chapter books count as 3` all PASSED in the same §1 that failed everything else. The counter was never involved.

## FIX 1 — THE INERT FIXTURE (§1 and §4, `test-cycle179-review-seq.php`)

**Cause, read from the code.** `tests/test-cycle179-review-seq.php` §0 called
`add_filter( 'bhp_school_visit_records', $bhp_rs_fake_visits, 99 )`.
There is no such filter. `bhp_school_visit_records()`
(`C:\BHP\brave-hearts-theme\plugins\brave-hearts-bundle-pricing\includes\school-visit-pickup.php`, line 946) reads `get_option( BHP_SCHOOL_VISIT_OPTION )`, sanitises each row and returns — **it contains no `apply_filters()` call at all.** `add_filter()` on an unused hook is silent and legal, so the fixture *looked* installed and injected nothing.

**Consequence, traced through the engine.** Probe visit absent → `bhp_review_ask_visit_date()` returns `''` → `bhp_review_ask_touch1_delay_days()` takes its documented unknown-visit branch into the **web** lane → 10 days, measured from **completion**, not 7 days from the visit date. That single fall-through produces, exactly, every failure Gandalf reported:

| Reported failure | Explained by |
|---|---|
| `ONE book on a visit order gives a 7-day delay -- got 10` | web delay substituted |
| `The visit-lane anchor is the visit date at local midnight, NOT the completion timestamp` | `bhp_review_ask_touch1_anchor()` fell through to `bhp_review_ask_anchor_timestamp()` |
| `visit + 7 ... DUE at nine days after the visit -- got: not_due` | order aged 9 days, needed 10 |
| `One-book visit order at visit+7 QUALIFIES -- got: not_due` | same |
| `Two-book visit order at visit+10 QUALIFIES -- got: not_due` | same |
| `one chapter book plus the Activity Book still gives a 7-day delay` | same |
| `and therefore keep the 7-day delay` | same |
| §4 `customer_cooldown -- got: not_due` | the cooldown sits **behind** the due-ness check, so a not-yet-due order never reaches it |

**Fix.** The fixture now hooks `pre_option_bhp_school_visits`, which is WordPress core and **is** applied — `get_option()` fires it before the cache and before the database. The plugin's own reader is left completely untouched, so all of its sanitising still runs on the probe row, which is the half worth exercising. Because `pre_option_` short-circuits, the **real** registry rows are read once, before the filter is added, and handed back alongside the probe; a replacement would have hidden every genuine visit from any concurrent staging request for the length of the run.

**Self-contained, and it proves itself.** §0 now (a) aborts loudly if `bhp_school_visit_records()` is absent instead of continuing into a meaningless pass, and (b) **calls the plugin's reader and asserts the probe came back** before any assertion depends on it. An inert hook now stops the suite with a named cause rather than reporting seven day-math regressions that are not regressions. §8 removes the filter and asserts two new things: that the real option is identical to its pre-run value, and that the probe slug was never written to it.

**⛔ NOTHING WAS WRITTEN TO `bhp_school_visits`.** The prior section's reasoning for not writing that option — it drives real checkout entitlement, and an aborted run would leave a real school broken — is correct and is preserved. The brief's instruction to "create their own registry entry" is met request-scoped, in memory, which is strictly safer than a write-and-restore.

## FIX 2 — CYCLE169 §5 (`Before opting out the order qualifies`)

**This is NOT pre-existing.** It is a real failure of that suite caused by seal 965, and 1.19.362 did not update this line with the rest. Two independent causes, both now stated in the file:

1. `$bhp_ra_opt` has no visit slug and no line items, so it is a **web-lane** order. Seal 965 ships web touch-1 copy as a PENDING-COPY placeholder with `approved => false`, a hard `copy_not_approved` decline. The order can no longer qualify outright whatever the opt-out ledger says.
2. §4's compatibility harness — which had been forcing the morning send window open — is released just above §5, so from that point the section's answer depended on the clock hour and would read `outside_send_window` outside 08:00–11:00 site-local.

**Fix.** Both gates are shimmed open through the engine's own public filters for that one assertion and released immediately inside §5. A **second** assertion was added that reads the *unshimmed* answer and requires it to be `copy_not_approved`, so the shim can never quietly mask a change in the copy gate. **Neither shim approves anything**: nothing in either suite approves copy, and `bhp_review_ask_copy_touch2()['approved']` is still asserted FALSE in §6 of the new suite.

## FIX 3 — DRY RUN WORKS WHILE THE ENGINE IS DISABLED

**Cause.** `bhp_review_ask_run()` halted on `! bhp_review_ask_is_enabled()` for **every** caller, dry included, which is why `wp bhp review-ask dry` answered *"engine is disabled … Nothing done"* for every `--as-of` date. The only way to see what the engine would do was to enable it — exactly backwards, and a real risk of the switch being thrown to read a preview, against real parents, with copy nobody has approved.

**Fix.** The disabled state now halts a **live** run and annotates a **dry** run. `$summary['halted']` still reads `disabled` and three log lines say so in words, so no reader can mistake a dry run for a live one. The `no_postal_address` halt behaves the same way, and continuing there is *more* informative: the per-order decline fires for every order, so the preview reads `0 would send / declined no_postal_address: N`.

**⛔ IT STILL CANNOT SEND — three independent structural reasons, any one sufficient.** (1) The dry branch `continue`s before `bhp_review_ask_send()` is reached, so the mailer is never constructed. (2) `bhp_review_ask_send()` now re-asserts the master switch itself, added in this build, as the last gate before a real parent. (3) The cron entry point `bhp_review_ask_cron_run()` is separately gated and never passes `dry`. Nothing on the dry path writes a marker, a cooldown stamp or a ledger row.

**Also:** `--as-of=<Y-m-d[ H:i:s]>` is now accepted on `dry` and **refused with an error on a live run** (a moved clock on a real run sends a future date's mail today and stamps a cooldown that never happened). A bare date is evaluated at 09:00 site-local, matching `plan`. `plan` now accepts `--as-of` as an alias for `--dates`, and prints whether the engine is enabled — it never was gated on the switch, correctly, but a plan read without that line looks like a schedule that is about to happen.

