## THE STAGING TEST-SEND COMMAND

```
wp bhp review-ask test-send --to=<address> --set=<day0|touch1|touch2|web1> --order=<id> [--dump=<path>] --url=https://staging2.braveheartspublishing.com
```

**Five refusals, all fail-closed, all before anything renders:**

1. **Staging only, and the check is on `home_url()` rather than on `--url`.** `--url` is what the operator *typed*; `home_url()` is which site WordPress actually *loaded*. A typo, a forgotten `--url` (which falls back to the primary site) or a multisite mapping would each put a test email in a real customer's mailbox from the production database. The command verifies the consequence, not the argument, and names both hosts in the refusal so the fix is obvious.
2. **`--to` is required, must be exactly one valid address, and there is no default.** It never falls back to the order's billing address. Commas and semicolons are refused.
3. **`--set` must be one of the four names.**
4. **`--order` must load as a real `WC_Order` on that database.**
5. **An `approved => false` set is refused, not previewed.** The gate exists so unapproved strings do not reach an inbox, and Gandalf's inbox is an inbox.

**Lane mismatch is refused rather than silently substituted.** `--set=web1` on a visit order errors and says which lane the order is on, because a preview that quietly shows you a different email than the one you asked for is worse than no preview.

**What it does NOT touch, which is the whole reason it is safe:** no `bhp_review_ask_log_send()`, no `bhp_review_ask_mark_sent()`, no `bhp_review_ask_record_customer()`, no `bhp_review_ask_record_optout()`, no `update_option()`. **No sent marker, no ledger row, no daily counter, no 90-day cooldown.** A test send today cannot make a real order ineligible tomorrow. It does not read or change the master switch, and it works with the switch OFF — a preview that needs the engine enabled is not a preview. The suite asserts the absence of all five calls by isolating the function body and searching it.

Subjects are prefixed **`[STAGING TEST]`**, and the mail carries `X-BHP-Test-Send: 1` and `Auto-Submitted: auto-generated`. A screenshot of an unprefixed review ask, forwarded on, is indistinguishable from a live send.

`--dump=<path>` also writes the rendered HTML to a file. A file can be opened in a browser and diffed; an inbox cannot.

**Day 0 goes through WooCommerce's own `customer_completed_order` object**, because that is what the theme's overlay actually decorates. Rendering it through the review-ask class would have proved nothing about what a parent receives.

**Suggested run, four commands per recipient** (substitute a real staging visit order id and a real staging web order id):

```
wp bhp review-ask test-send --set=day0   --order=<visit id> --to=<you> --url=https://staging2.braveheartspublishing.com
wp bhp review-ask test-send --set=touch1 --order=<visit id> --to=<you> --url=https://staging2.braveheartspublishing.com
wp bhp review-ask test-send --set=touch2 --order=<visit id> --to=<you> --url=https://staging2.braveheartspublishing.com
wp bhp review-ask test-send --set=web1   --order=<web id>   --to=<you> --url=https://staging2.braveheartspublishing.com
```

⚠ **Run `wp bhp review-ask status` first.** It now reports the day-0 set's approval alongside the other three, so the four APPROVED lines are the cheapest proof the deploy landed.

## TESTS WRITTEN

`tests/test-cycle179-review-seq.php` §6 was **inverted, not deleted, and the inversion is the point.** It used to prove "PENDING-COPY can never be sent" by observing two sets that happened to be unapproved. Those sets are now approved, so the old assertions would assert the wrong world. **The gate is proved the other way round instead: the section forces a set unapproved through the engine's own public `bhp_review_ask_copy` filter and asserts the decline still fires, then removes the shim and asserts the set reports approved again.** A gate observed to fire on demand is proved; a gate that merely happened to be closed was only ever being described.

New **§6B**, Merry's prose asserted word for word against the **unmerged** sets, so a slot that fails to resolve cannot make a sentence look right by disappearing: every V2 §3 and §4 line · touch 2 carrying **no `you`, `your` or `busy`** in its opening sentence (seal 977's whole point) · **there is no touch 3**, which is what makes *"the last note I will send"* true · the seal-981 sentence absent from every shipped set · the generic swap's three lines and the absence of any `{ChildFirstName}` slot in it · **the +7 and +10 openers differing from each other** · the star-row instruction verbatim on all three sets · usability, no-Amazon, no-em-dash and subject-length on each.

New **§6C**: `test-send` is dispatched, checks `home_url()`, and its isolated function body calls **none** of the five ledger/option writers.

Two day-0 assertions were inverted the same way, and the gate re-proved against an unknown set key.

## ⛔ WHAT IS NOT VERIFIED — STATED PLAINLY

**There is no PHP interpreter on this machine** (`which php` → nothing, re-checked this session). **`php -l` was NOT run. No line of this code has been parsed by an interpreter. No suite was executed. No staging deploy, no browser, no email was rendered or sent by me.**

What I did run is a **PHP-aware bracket balance** over all three edited files — a single-pass scanner that tracks single quotes, double quotes, heredocs, `//`, `#` and `/* */` so that a `//` inside a URL string cannot corrupt it. All three edited files balance at `(0, 0, 0)`, **and so do the three pre-edit backups run through the same scanner as a control**, which is what makes the result mean anything. ⛔ **It is a bracket count, not a lint.** It would not catch a missing semicolon, a bad signature or an undefined function.

Specifically unproven and most likely to bite:

- **Nobody has seen any of these four emails.** That is what `test-send` exists for and it is the next thing that should happen.
- **`test-send` itself has never run.** Its staging refusal cannot be proved by a suite without running the command on a non-staging host, which is the exact thing the refusal exists to prevent. **A human should run it once with a wrong `--url` and read the refusal.**
- **`$email->style_inline( $email->get_content() )` on the WooCommerce completed-order object for `--set=day0` is reasoned from WooCommerce's API, not observed.** If it throws, day 0 is the one of the four that fails.
- The named/generic branch depends on `bhp_review_ask_child_first_name()`, whose meta keys are still **UNVERIFIED CANDIDATES** in the source. On staging it will almost certainly take the generic branch, which is the correct default but means **the named variant may go unseen in QA.** Force it with a test order carrying one of the candidate meta keys if Andrew wants to see it.

