# ROUND 5 — 1.19.366 · CYCLE179-LD-REVIEW-SEQ-R5

**lead-developer (Aragorn), subagent of chief-of-staff (Gandalf) · 2026-09-05 · lock `lead-developer-CYCLE179-LD-REVIEW-SEQ-R5.lock`**

Objective: fix the eight `test-cycle179-review-seq` failures and the four
`test-cycle169-review-ask` failures Gandalf measured on staging at 1.19.365, **at
root cause, without loosening a single assertion**; and apply Andrew's pending
*"fix the review page"* to `template-parts/reviews/review-form.php`.

## THE FOUR ROOT CAUSES, EACH STATED

### 1. The visit set encoded a WORDING VARIANT as a SET IDENTITY

`bhp_review_ask_copy_visit_touch1()` returned
`$named ? 'visit_touch1' : 'visit_touch1_generic'`. `set` is the copy set's
identity — the thing seal 982 approved, the thing the CLI run summary prints,
the thing any future ledger row would key on. The named/generic swap is two
wordings of **one** approved set (V2 §2 supplies both under one heading), not
two sets. So the branch that **most real orders take** (CYCLE179-MKT-32: there
is no `{ChildFirstName}` WooCommerce field) was naming a set key that no
approval, no gate and no summary has ever heard of.

**Fixed at the producer:** `set` is now always `'visit_touch1'`; the wording is
reported on a new `'variant'` key (`named` / `generic`) that nothing gates on.
The CLI summary prints it beside the set. This one change fixed **three**
assertions across two suites — `test-cycle179-review-seq` §7, and both
`test-cycle169-review-ask` compatibility-harness lines, which ask a no-order
preview to name its set.

### 2. The day-0 approval gate answered about the FALLBACK, not the key asked

`bhp_visit_email_copy()` substitutes the `_default` set for any slug it does not
carry. That is **correct for rendering** — a real order must still get an email
— and **catastrophic for an approval question**, which is about a named set and
nothing else. `bhp_visit_email_copy_is_approved()` read `approved` off whatever
came back.

⛔ **It looked correct only by luck.** While `_default` was itself unapproved,
the fallback happened to return false for an unknown key. **Seal 982 approved
`_default`, and the same unchanged code then reported every unknown slug on the
site as approved copy.** The suite caught it with `no-such-set-ever-2026`.

**Fixed:** the gate now detects that an unknown slug was *substituted for*
rather than *answered* and returns false. The `bhp_visit_email_copy` filter seam
survives untouched — a filter supplying real strings for a slug this theme does
not carry returns something other than the default set, and that set's own flag
governs. ⚠ `bhp_visit_email_may_render()` carried the **identical** defect on
the path that reaches parents; it now asks the same function.

### 3. The pre-fill fixture was force-deleted before it was asked about

`test-cycle179-review-seq` §9 was appended in 1.19.364 **after** §8's teardown,
and the teardown was not moved with it. §9.7 minted a genuine token for
`$bhp_rs_v1` and then asked `bhp_review_prefill()` for the name box — for an
order already deleted from the database.

⭐ **The engine was right the whole time.** The name is deliberately **not** in
the token (so it cannot be forged); it is looked up through `wc_get_order()` and
returns `''` when the order is gone. Deleting the fixture and then demanding the
name is asking a correct function to answer for something that no longer exists.

**Fixed:** the assertion is **unchanged** — it still demands the literal
`'Testparent'` *from the order rather than from the token*. The teardown moved
to a new §11 that runs after §9 and §10, and now additionally **verifies** no
probe order survives rather than only counting the deletions.

### 4. Two suites pinned superseded copy

**`test-cycle179-review-seq` §7** pinned `CYCLE179-MKT-REVIEW-ASKS.md` §1, which
1.19.365 superseded with V2 §2 under seal 982. Per Gandalf's ruling on
**CYCLE179-LD-47**, the pins now read V2 §2. Five ASKS §1 pins removed and
**named in the file** so a supersession cannot be mistaken for an accident, then
**re-asserted the other way round**: restoring that prose from an older draft
now fails the suite. The **named** wording is pinned for the first time.

**`test-cycle169-review-ask` §5**: `$bhp_ra_opt` carried no line items, so it
could not resolve `{BookTitle}` or `{ReviewLink}` and declined
`unresolved_merge_slot` before the opt-out — the variable under test — was ever
reached. The fixture predates 1.19.364, which repaired
`bhp_review_ask_merge_is_complete()` after it had been **structurally dead since
it was written**. `bhp_ra_make_order()` now takes an optional `$book_ids` (every
existing caller unchanged) and this order carries a chapter book, asserted.

Its second assertion expected `copy_not_approved` **unshimmed**, citing seal
965's PENDING-COPY placeholder. Seal 982 approved the web set, so that reason
can no longer fire and keeping the expectation would have asserted that Andrew's
approval had not happened. It now asserts **the true current state**: the order
qualifies unshimmed, which makes the section's copy shim **provably a no-op** —
a stronger statement, not a looser one. The hard copy gate is proved separately
on a set forced unapproved, so `approved` still demonstrably means something.

## THE REVIEW PAGE (Andrew: *"fix the review page"*)

⚠ **Gandalf's line numbers were stale.** The brief named lines 301 and 325; the
two strings are at **367** and **391** in `template-parts/reviews/review-form.php`
at 1.19.365. Line 301 is inside a `sprintf()` and 325 is the textarea
placeholder — editing by number would have hit the wrong strings. Located by
content instead.

Both superseded strings are **preserved in comments**, no other copy touched,
**star labels unchanged** (they are Andrew's approved wording and two of them end
*"not for us"* / *"did not work for us"*, which is why the brief specified
"we" and not "us"):

| | Was | Now |
|---|---|---|
| privacy | *"…mailing list **—** it is only how **we** can reach you about your review."* | *"…mailing list. It is only for a reply about your review."* |
| moderation | *"Nothing is edited **—** reviews are either…"* | *"Nothing is edited**:** reviews are either…"* |

**New §10** asserts on the **rendered** page (not the template source): no em
dash, no en dash, no standalone *"we"* on the stripped visible text, both
replacement sentences pinned verbatim, neither superseded sentence surviving,
and — because *"Every review is read before it appears"* is a claim about
behaviour — that the `pre_comment_approved` moderation hold is actually wired.

⛔ **What §10 deliberately does NOT cover, stated rather than quietly excluded:**
1. **Approved customer review text.** `bhp_review_render_section()` renders real
   parents' sentences. Those are not the store's copy and the store does not get
