# ROUND 6 — 1.19.367 — the two §10 failures, fixed at root cause

**Lead Developer (Aragorn) · 2026-09-05 · CYCLE179-LD-REVIEW-SEQ R6 · from Gandalf**

## OBJECTIVE AND ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

Clear the two remaining `test-cycle179-review-seq` §10 failures at 1.19.366
(`No em dash anywhere in the rendered review page`; `The email-privacy line is
the approved 1.19.366 wording, verbatim`), state the root cause of each,
replace the four named em-dash UI strings, sweep the whole reviews surface for
any remaining U+2014 / U+2013 in customer-facing copy, bump to 1.19.367, ship a
gated ZIP, commit locally, release the lock.

## ⭐⭐ THE TWO ROOT CAUSES — BOTH REAL, NEITHER WHERE IT LOOKED

Gandalf's curl and the test suite were **both correct**. They disagreed because
they were reading different things.

### 1. The em dash — a `<script type="application/json">` block, not prose

`template-parts/reviews/review-form.php` prints the whole of
`bhp_review_error_messages()` as JSON into
`<script type="application/json" class="bhp-review-form__messages">` **on every
page load**, so `assets/js/reviews.js` can show validation messages
client-side. Two of those strings carried em dashes (`email_invalid`,
`generic` — `inc/reviews.php`).

§10 asserts on the **raw** `$bhp_rs_page` and saw them. Gandalf's curl stripped
tags, and `wp_strip_all_tags()` deletes `<script>` blocks **content and all**
(it runs a `preg_replace` over `<script>…</script>` and `<style>…</style>`
before `strip_tags()`), so the stripped text never contained them. That same
mechanism is why the no-`we` check passed while sitting on top of a JSON string
that contains a standalone "we" — see CYCLE179-LD-53 below.

**The raw check is the correct one.** Those strings are displayed to reviewers
by JS, so they are customer-facing copy under Standing Rules 608.

**⭐ VERIFIED LIVE, not inferred.** `curl` of
`https://staging2.braveheartspublishing.com/review/the-mariana-trench/` at
1.19.366 (HTTP 200, 176 400 bytes): **21 em dashes in the whole file, exactly
one of them below the `<head>` scripts — line 1121, the JSON messages block,
inside the form at lines 1097–1262. Zero en dashes anywhere in the file.**

### 2. The privacy pin — the suite runs `--user=1`, the sentence is logged-out-only

`template-parts/reviews/review-form.php:325` wraps the name field, the email
field **and the privacy sentence** in `if ( ! is_user_logged_in() )`, closing at
line 377. The suite's own documented invocation (test file header, line 9) is
`--user=1`. Under a logged-in user that entire block never renders, so the
pinned sentence was **genuinely absent from what §10 read** — while being
present and byte-correct for every real reader.

The moderation line sits **outside** that guard (line ~403), which is exactly
why its twin passed and this one did not.

**It was NOT** entity encoding of the apostrophe-free sentence, **NOT**
whitespace collapsing, **NOT** `esc_html` output of `.` versus the pin, and
**NOT** the pin reading the wrong element. All four hypotheses are ruled out by
the live render: line 1241 of the staging HTML carries the approved sentence
character for character.

**Fix:** §10 now drops the current user to `0` for the render and restores it
immediately (§11's teardown needs those capabilities), so the section asserts
the state a buyer is actually in. Nobody arrives from a review-ask email logged
in as the shop administrator. Two new assertions prove the drop happened and
the restore happened.

## EVERY EM/EN DASH FOUND IN A CUSTOMER-FACING STRING, AND WHAT IT IS NOW

Superseded wording is preserved in a comment at every site.

