# ROUND 7 — 1.19.368 · CYCLE179-LD-53 CLOSED, AND THE `--dump` INVOCATIONS

**Aragorn (lead-developer), 2026-09-05.** Commit `a39c913` on
`feature/cycle179-review-seq-1.19.362`. Lock
`lead-developer-CYCLE179-LD-REVIEW-SEQ-R7.lock`, released on completion.

## The one customer-facing defect, closed

`bhp_review_error_messages()['author']` read *"Please add your name, so we know
who the review is from."* Standing Rules 9.1 forbids the store speaking as a
person, and this string is not a comment: `assets/js/reviews.js` shows it to a
reviewer who submits without a name. It now reads:

> **"Please add your name, so the review has a name on it."**

The promise is unchanged; only the voice moved. The superseded sentence is
**preserved in the docblock at its own site**, not deleted, so an older build
cannot restore it unnoticed.

### Why 1.19.367 shipped with it — the same blind spot, one release later

§10's `"we"` assertion ran on `wp_strip_all_tags()` output. That function
deletes `<script>` blocks **content and all**, and these strings are printed
into `<script type="application/json" class="bhp-review-form__messages">`. So
the only `"we"` left on the page was invisible to the only test looking for it.
**That is exactly the 1.19.366 em-dash failure, in the same block, one release
later.** Fixing the string without fixing the test would have left the trap
armed for the next person.

## The `we / us / our` sweep

`inc/reviews.php` and `assets/js/reviews.js`, word-boundary, case-insensitive.
**Every hit is reported; only the store's own voice was changed.**

| Site | Text | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| `inc/reviews.php:330` `'author'` | "so **we** know who the review is from" | ⛔ **FIXED** — the store speaking, shown to reviewers by JS. This is CYCLE179-LD-53. |
| `inc/reviews.php:196` and `:464` honeypot `wp_die()` | "Sorry, **we** could not accept that submission." | ⛔ **FIXED** → "Sorry, that submission could not be accepted." **NEW in this sweep — not named in the brief, found by it.** ⚠ Bot-only by design (a human never trips the honeypot), but it is still rendered copy in the store's voice, so it was not left standing on a technicality. |
| `inc/reviews.php:1077` `'2 stars: not for **us**'` | reader's voice | ⭐ **LEFT.** Andrew's approved copy; §9.1 pins all five star labels verbatim. |
| `inc/reviews.php:1078` `'1 star: did not work for **us**'` | reader's voice | ⭐ **LEFT**, same reason. |
| `inc/reviews.php` lines 237, 266, 283, 316-322, 510, 513, 521, 529, 846 | "ours", "our two forms", "we get the reason", plus the preserved superseded wording | ⭐ **LEFT.** All inside `/* */` or `//` comments. Code commentary is not customer-facing — the same ruling Gandalf made on the four em dashes in the consent-banner CLS guard. |
| `assets/js/reviews.js` (333 lines) | — | ⭐ **ZERO HITS.** File unchanged. |

## §10, extended at both ends

- The JSON messages block is now pulled out of the **raw** `$bhp_rs_page` by
  regex and scanned for a standalone `"we"`, for em/en dashes, for the approved
  replacement **verbatim**, and for the **absence** of the superseded sentence.
- ⛔ **A must-be-able-to-fail guard runs first**, asserting the block is
  actually present in the render. Without it the scan would pass on an empty
  corpus and report a clean page it never read — the same tautology failure the
  R6 in-round correction caught.
- A **direct scan of `bhp_review_error_messages()`** sits beside the existing
  dash loop and **names the offending key** when it goes red, which a render
  scan cannot. Both are kept: one for proof, one for diagnosis.
- The `⚠ RAISED, NOT RESOLVED — CYCLE179-LD-53` note is now `⭐ CLOSED IN 1.19.368`.

### ⚠ One design choice recorded rather than made silently

The brief says "assert the raw render". I did **not** write
`preg_match('/\bwe\b/i', $bhp_rs_page)` over the raw HTML, and the reason is in
a comment at the site: `\b` treats a hyphen as a word boundary, so **any**
class, data attribute, query arg or inline third-party script carrying a `-we-`
or `_we_` token fails it, on markup nobody reads as copy. That is a flaky test,
not a stronger one — and a test that cries wolf on correct code is the more
expensive failure, exactly as this project's own RUNBOOK says about the
`MUST be 10` minified-CSS gate. The raw render is instead read with **tags
removed but script content kept**, narrowed to the block that has now caught
fire twice. Attributes cannot reach it; the strings that matter cannot escape
it. **If Gandalf wants the blunt version anyway, say so and it goes in — but it
should go in knowing it may go red on markup.**

`"us"` is still deliberately not asserted anywhere in §10, for the star-label
reason above.

## ⭐ THE `test-send --dump` INVOCATIONS FOR STAGING

Signature (`inc/review-ask-email.php:4035`), read out of the shipped file:

```
wp bhp review-ask test-send --to=<address> --set=<day0|touch1|touch2|web1> --order=<id> [--dump=<path>]
```

### Step 1 — find a staging order that carries a chapter book (READ-ONLY)

```bash
wp wc shop_order list --user=1 --per_page=20 --fields=id,status,line_items \
  --url=https://staging2.braveheartspublishing.com
```

⚠ **This store runs HPOS** — orders live in `wc_orders`, not `wp_posts`. A
`wp post list --post_type=shop_order` returns **0** and has already produced a
false "no orders" claim in a session report. The command above is the correct
one and it writes nothing.

Pick an id whose `line_items` names one of the three chapter books (Mariana
Trench / Mount Everest / The Amazon, paperback or hardcover). Confirm the
engine agrees before spending a send — the command prints the lane and the
count itself on its first two lines:

```
order:     #<id> | <visit|web> lane | <n> chapter book(s)
```

If it prints `0 chapter book(s)`, that order will not exercise the merge slots;
pick another.

### Step 2 — the four sends

Substitute `<ORDER>` once and `<YOU>` once.

```bash
cd <staging_doc_root>

wp bhp review-ask test-send --set=day0   --order=<ORDER> --to=<YOU> \
  --dump=/tmp/bhp-review-ask-day0.html   --url=https://staging2.braveheartspublishing.com

wp bhp review-ask test-send --set=touch1 --order=<ORDER> --to=<YOU> \
  --dump=/tmp/bhp-review-ask-touch1.html --url=https://staging2.braveheartspublishing.com

wp bhp review-ask test-send --set=touch2 --order=<ORDER> --to=<YOU> \
  --dump=/tmp/bhp-review-ask-touch2.html --url=https://staging2.braveheartspublishing.com

wp bhp review-ask test-send --set=web1   --order=<ORDER> --to=<YOU> \
  --dump=/tmp/bhp-review-ask-web1.html   --url=https://staging2.braveheartspublishing.com
```

Each prints `dumped:    <path> (<n> bytes)`. **Grep the dumps, do not only look
at the inbox** — this is the whole point of `--dump`:

```bash
grep -c $'\xe2\x80\x94\|\xe2\x80\x93' /tmp/bhp-review-ask-*.html   # em/en dash: expect 0
grep -nEi '\bwe\b'                    /tmp/bhp-review-ask-*.html   # standalone "we": expect nothing
grep -c  'STAGING TEST'               /tmp/bhp-review-ask-*.html   # expect 1 each
```

### ⛔ What these commands will and will not do — read before running

- **Staging only, and it cannot be argued past.** The gate is on `home_url()`,
  not on `--url`: a typo or a missing `--url` refuses rather than mailing from
  the production database.
- **`--to` is the only recipient.** No default, no fallback to the order's
  billing address, no bcc. One address per invocation.
- **Nothing is written.** No sent marker, no ledger row, no daily counter, no
  90-day customer cooldown. A test send today cannot make a real order
  ineligible tomorrow.
- **The master switch is neither read nor changed** — this works with the
  engine OFF, which is how it should be run.
- **The copy gate still applies.** An `approved => false` set is **REFUSED**,
  not previewed. ⚠ Per `wp bhp review-ask status`, the `web touch 1` and
  `touch 2` copy sets were reported as pending approval; if either has not been
  approved since, that invocation will refuse. **That is the gate working, not
  a bug** — run `wp bhp review-ask status` first and read the four `copy` lines.
- Subjects arrive prefixed `[STAGING TEST]`.

