## 1. STAR BLOCK REBUILD — seal 998

**Andrew, verbatim:** *"The stars look terrible, they should show up just link
an amazon review. 5 stars in a row from left to right."*

**What ships.** One centred row of **five identical gold star PNGs**, ascending
left to right. Star 1 → `?rating=1` … star 5 → `?rating=5`, the same five URLs
as 1.19.368 carried, reordered. Each star is its own `<a>`.

- **Image:** `assets/images/email/review-star-gold@2x.png`, **64 × 64**,
  rendered at **32 × 32** via `width`/`height` attributes *and* inline CSS.
  Brand gold `#D9A45F` (`--color-gold`) filled, `#805800` (`--color-gold-deep`,
  the documented light-ground gold) edged, transparent ground. Generated in
  this build with Pillow at 8× supersample and Lanczos downsample.
  **⚠ The edge is there for a reason and it is not decoration:** `#D9A45F` on
  white is roughly 2.2:1, below the 3:1 non-text minimum; the deep-gold edge is
  what makes the shape legible against a white or cream email ground.
  **⚠ That ratio is computed, not measured with a contrast tool.**
- **Alt text:** `"1 star"`, `"2 stars"` … `"5 stars"` — from
  `bhp_review_ask_star_alt()`. **Not** the site form's descriptive labels: the
  alt of a link *is* its accessible name, and five names reading "5 stars:
  loved it" turns a rating control into five competing pitches.
- **Markup:** an outer 100%-wide presentation table with one centred cell,
  holding an inner `table-layout:fixed` table of five cells. Outlook's Word
  renderer ignores flex and grid; a `<div>` row collapses to five stacked lines.
- **Tap target:** 32px image + 6px padding on every side = **44 × 44**.
  ⚠ **Computed from the declared sizes; no email client was opened.**
- **Caption, in body text, centred, not bold:**
  *"Tap a star to rate {BookTitle}. Then two or three honest sentences on the
  next page."* New copy key `stars_caption`, on all three live sets.
- **`links_lead` is now empty** on all three sets. Its old string (*"Tap the
  stars that fit…"*) rendered inside `<strong>`, which 1.19.365 itself flagged
  as a rendering deviation from Merry's page. Both templates already guarded it
  conditionally, so nothing prints an empty `<strong>`.
- **The bare title link is gone.** `copy_is_usable()` **does** require a
  non-empty `links` array, so the brief's second option was taken: the label is
  now **"Or open the review page"** and it renders centred, small and unbolded.
  The `white-space:nowrap` mobile fix from 2026-08-29 is **kept for the
  three-title legacy row and deliberately not applied to the single link** —
  five unbreakable words at 375px would push the line off the side, which is
  that rule inverted.
- **Plain text:** five lines, `1 star: <url>` … `5 stars: <url>`, ascending,
  then the caption.

**Why an image at all, beyond the ruling.** U+2605 is substituted by Gmail's
Android and iOS clients with a **colour emoji** and by several Outlook builds
with a **box**, so "a row of five stars" was never reliably a row of five stars
in the two clients that matter most.

**The blocked-image objection, answered rather than ignored.** 1.19.364's
comment argued hard for glyphs (*"a row of five broken-image icons with no
labels is a dead end"*) and that comment is preserved verbatim at its own site.
With images blocked the reader now gets five underlined text links reading
"1 star" … "5 stars", plus the caption in body text, plus "Or open the review
page". There is no state in which the block is a dead end.

**⚠ THE REAL TRADE, REPORTED NOT HIDDEN.** The rating each star carries is no
longer stated in **visible text** beside it for a sighted reader with images on.
It survives in the alt attribute and in the plain part. That is how Amazon's own
row works and it is what seal 998 asked for; it is nonetheless less explicit
than what it replaced. **Merry's V2 §5 rule 1 ("nothing steers toward five") is
satisfied more strictly than before**, not less: every cell is now the same
image, same size, same padding, same target, no default, no highlight.

## 2. DAY 0 — one generic set for every visit — seal 994

- `bhp_visit_email_copy()` **no longer looks the slug up**. It returns the
  `_default` set for every slug. The superseded lookup is preserved in a comment
