## 5. MIGRATION AND THE CAP

**The sixteen are ordinary visit orders.** `wp bhp review-ask migrate` now
**refuses 612, 615, 620, 621, 624, 628, 634, 654, 716, 717, 730, 732, 737, 741,
770, 772 by id**, on a dry run as well as an apply, from
`bhp_review_ask_migration_retired_orders()` (filterable). The command is kept,
unused and documented, for a genuinely hand-sent ask.

**⛔ WHY THE REFUSAL IS THE POINT.** `external-pending-<date>` writes the SENT
marker. One `--apply` against the list an earlier round produced would suppress
touch 1 **forever** and make touch 2 decline `touch1_date_unknown` — cancelling
the entire launch, with nothing downstream saying so. The run summary would just
report sixteen orders already marked.

**The cap: BOTH, and here is which.** Raised to **10** *and* made **per lane**.

- **The 10** answers *"must not delay any of them"*. The four touch-1 dates plus
  touch 2 four days later mean **2026-09-14 carries two groups at once** (Dallas
  one-book touch 2 **and** Liberty multi touch 1).
  **⚠ I did not read the per-school split of the sixteen off either
  environment.** 10 is sized for the worst plausible split of 16 across four
  dates, not measured against the real one. **If Gandalf's dry runs show more
  than ten on any single date, raise it for that day via the filter** — the dry
  run will say so by name (`daily_cap_lane`).
- **Per lane** answers what 10 alone does not: on switch-on morning every past
  web order older than 14 days qualifies at once, and a shared cap would let
  that backlog eat the budget before the visit orders — the ones with a promised
  date — were reached. Counted from the send log's existing `lane` field; no new
  option row, no back-fill. A row with no `lane` counts against **both** lanes
  (over-counting delays an email; under-counting sends one).
- **A cap of 0 now means 0.** The old line replaced 0 with the default, so an
  operator who filtered the cap to zero believing they had paused a lane would
  have got five sends. This can only make the engine send **fewer** emails.
- An exhausted lane **skips its orders by name** (`daily_cap_lane`) instead of
  ending the run, so the other lane still gets its morning. `halted =>
  daily_cap_reached` now means *every* lane is exhausted.

