# ROUND 9 — 1.19.370 — charset, the Unicode star row, the hero shell

- **Role:** `lead-developer` (Aragorn), subagent of `chief-of-staff` (Gandalf)
- **Date:** 2026-09-05, ~13:2x to ~13:4x MDT
- **Lock:** `Business OS\LOCKS\lead-developer-CYCLE179-LD-REVIEW-SEQ-R9.lock`
- **Branch:** `feature/cycle179-review-seq-1.19.362`
- **Design authority read in full:** `Business OS\WORKING-DRAFTS\design-creative\CYCLE179-DES-REVIEW-EMAIL.md` (2026-09-05)

## 1. Objective and acceptance criteria

Five items from Gandalf's round-9 brief, all founder-driven: the charset on every
engine email; the star row rebuilt as Unicode with a grey resting state and a gold
hover; the shell per Legolas's spec including hero photographs and a signature
block; the two failing assertions fixed at root cause; tests for all of it, at
1.19.370, packaged with gates and a deliverable.

## 2. Implementation decisions, and why

### 2.1 The charset — one filter, not four

`bhp_email_force_charset()` on `woocommerce_email_headers`, in
`inc\transactional-emails.php`. **Chosen over editing `WC_Email_BHP_Review_Ask::get_headers()`
because `woocommerce_email_headers` fires for every WooCommerce email**, and the
brief names two of them: the review ask, and the visit day-0 email — which is
`customer_completed_order` with the school-visit body fork, not a separate class.
One filter covers both, plus every receipt the store already sends. The mojibake
defect was never specific to the star row.

**It only adds.** An existing `charset` is left exactly as it is and the media type
is never rewritten, so a `multipart/alternative` or `text/plain` email keeps its
type and merely gains the parameter. Rewriting a media type from a header filter
is how a plain-text email starts arriving as HTML source. Matching is done line by
line on the `Content-Type` header name so a `text/html` substring inside a
`List-Unsubscribe` URL is not caught — asserted.

**Why WordPress did not already handle it:** `wp_mail()` reads the charset from the
`Content-Type` header and otherwise falls back to `get_bloginfo('charset')`, but
FluentSMTP replaces `wp_mail()` wholesale. A header that states its charset does
not depend on any mailer's fallback being the one we want.

### 2.2 The star row — the PNG decision reversed, on evidence

Round 8 chose PNGs and argued the case well. **Legolas disproved the load-bearing
half of that argument by rendering it:** with images unavailable the row produced
"a row of empty grey boxes", not five fallback text links. Outlook desktop blocks
images by default. That is a dead end on the single client most likely to receive
this email, so the mark is a character again.

The round-8 counter-argument is **preserved in the template, not deleted**: Gmail
mobile substitutes a colour emoji for U+2605 and some Outlook builds draw a box.
Both are accepted. A colour star is still a star; a box still carries the link,
the `aria-label` and the caption. Only the empty grey box is a dead end.

**Hover, and the trick that was refused.** The usual email cumulative-fill trick is
to reverse the DOM and set `direction: rtl`. **Refused**, because seal 998 is
specifically about the row reading 1 to 5 left to right and that would stake the
one thing the founder asked for on Outlook's Word renderer honouring `dir="rtl"`.
Instead the DOM stays ascending and `:has()` does the work:
`.bhp-star:has(~ .bhp-star:hover) a` golds 1..N-1 and `.bhp-star a:hover` golds N.
Where `:has()` is unsupported the reader gets star N alone — the brief's stated
minimum. Where the `<style>` block is stripped the row rests grey and every link
still works.

The resting grey is **also inline** on each link, so a webmail that strips the
`<style>` block still gets the intended resting state and loses only the hover.

### 2.3 The hero band

`bhp_review_ask_hero( $order, $context )`, mapping filterable via
`bhp_review_ask_hero_map`, keyed on `_bhp_school_visit_slug` with a reserved
`general` key. Dallas Harris to frame 02 on touch 1 (the book being read), frame 01
on day 0 (the whole room), per Legolas §7. **Any unmapped slug and every web order
gets the general frame**, because a photograph captioned for a school the family
never attended is a false statement in a picture. **Touch 2 returns an empty array.**
The file is checked on disk before a URL is emitted, so a filter naming an
undeployed file renders nothing rather than a broken-image icon.

Day 0 is gated on the visit body being non-empty — **an ordinary web receipt gets
no photograph.**

### 2.4 Three deviations from the design spec, recorded not absorbed

**(a) The hero renders BELOW the H1, not above it.** Legolas §6 puts it above.
Doing that requires overriding `emails/email-header.php`, and
`inc\transactional-emails.php` carries a standing prohibition on exactly that: the
`email_improvements` feature flag is enabled on this store and rewrites both
header and footer, so an override pins the theme to one branch of core and
diverges silently on the next update. **A layout preference does not outrank a rule
written to stop a silent break on a core update.** It is moot on every email this
build actually sends: the H1 is empty in all three live copy sets, so the hero is
the first thing under the logo band regardless. It matters only if an admin types
a heading into wp-admin.

**(b) No Facebook or Instagram link is emitted, and this is the one item that needs
someone else to act.** Legolas §12 names "Facebook · Instagram". **No such URL exists
anywhere in this repository.** Grepped 2026-09-05 across `inc\`, `functions.php`,
`woocommerce\`, `template-parts\` and the content engine; the only `facebook.com`
hits are the Meta pixel endpoint and Meta's own privacy-policy URL. **A
plausible-looking profile URL is a fabricated fact and was not invented.** The
signature block ends on the brand line. Supply the two real URLs through the
`bhp_review_ask_social_links` filter or the `bhp_social_links` option and the line
appears with no deploy. A test asserts **both** that no `facebook.com` or
`instagram.com` string is emitted today **and** that two supplied links render.

**(c) The H1 stays empty.** Legolas §6 lists an H1 in all four emails but supplies
no text for it, and the copy authority has none. Filling it was tried and rejected
on a render at 1.19.36x: the reader met the same sentence three times in the first
screen. **Inventing an H1 string would be writing copy into an email whose copy is
locked.** The 30px sizing from §5 is applied; the string is not invented.

### 2.5 The signature block

Built as asked, and **flagged as `CYCLE179-DES-29(a)`, still Andrew's call.** It
sits below a rule after the P.S. so the letter still ends where the approved copy
ends; the consequence is that the name appears twice, once as the sign-off and
once as furniture. Rendered in both the HTML and the plain part, because anything
present in one and absent from the other is drift.

### 2.6 The two failing assertions — the engine was right both times

**§6 web gate, `not_due` instead of `copy_not_approved`.** The web probe order was
built 12 days old; 1.19.369 raised `BHP_REVIEW_ASK_WEB_DELAY_DAYS` to 14 on
Andrew's ruling and the fixture was never moved. The order was genuinely not due.
Aged to **16, not 14** — at exactly 14 the order becomes due at local midnight, so a
run in the small hours could land either side of the boundary and the file would
fail intermittently for a reason unrelated to what it tests.

**§9.6 "The 2-star link carries rating=2".** It read offset 3 of the row — correct
under the descending row (5,4,3,2,1), wrong the moment 1.19.369 made the row
ascending, where offset 3 is the 4-star link. **The fix is not offset 1.** An offset
is the wrong way to ask the question; it re-breaks on the next reorder. The row is
now **searched for rating 2**, and the ascending order is asserted separately and on
purpose, by comparing the positions of the five `aria-label` strings in the
emitted HTML.

