first-hand): *"I like the nice signature and big place brave hearts - drop the
plain one"*.

The standalone `Andrew` sign-off is removed from **all four sets in the sequence**:

| Set | File | Was | Is |
|---|---|---|---|
| day 0 | `inc\visit-completed-email.php` | body of 9 paragraphs, last one `Andrew` | body of 8, ends on the reply route |
| visit touch 1 | `inc\review-ask-email.php` | `signoff => array( 'Andrew' )` | `signoff => array()` |
| web touch 1 | `inc\review-ask-email.php` | `signoff => array( 'Andrew' )` | `signoff => array()` |
| touch 2 | `inc\review-ask-email.php` | `signoff => array( 'Andrew' )` | `signoff => array()` |

Every superseded line is preserved **verbatim in a comment at its own site**, with
the seal, the date and the relay caveat. **The eyebrow stays omitted.**

⛔ **The legacy 21-day set was NOT touched.** `bhp_review_ask_copy_legacy_21day()`
also carries a plain sign-off (`Andrew` / `Brave Hearts Publishing` plus the
tagline), but it is the superseded pre-sequence copy and the brief names four sets,
not five. If Andrew's edit is meant to reach it too, that is a one-line follow-up —
**flagged, not assumed.**

### 1a. The defect this edit would have caused if the gate had not moved with it

`bhp_review_ask_copy_is_usable()` required `signoff` to be **non-empty**. Emptying
the three arrays without touching that check would have made every approved set
fail its own usability test and the engine fall back to a set nobody selected —
**with no error anywhere, and only where it matters.** `signoff` now sits in the
present-and-must-be-an-array group beside `question`, `links_lead` and
`body_middle` (the same relaxation those got on 2026-09-05, for the same reason),
so a typo'd or deleted key is still caught. §14.5 asserts each of the three sets is
**still usable** with an empty signoff. This is the single highest-risk line in the
round and it is tested directly.

## 2. Item 2 — CHARSET ON THE WIRE

**Why 1.19.370 was not enough.** `bhp_email_force_charset()` writes the parameter
into the header **string** handed to `wp_mail()`. FluentSMTP replaces `wp_mail()`
wholesale: it parses that string into its own structures and re-emits headers.
Anything not carried across the parse is lost — which is consistent with log ids
6 and 7 still reading bare `text/html` (⚠ **relayed by Gandalf, not observed here**).

Built:

- **`bhp_email_phpmailer_charset()` on `phpmailer_init`, priority 99**
  (`inc\transactional-emails.php`). Sets `$phpmailer->CharSet` from
  `get_bloginfo('charset')`, defaulting UTF-8. That property is not a header to be
  parsed — it is what PHPMailer and everything built on it uses to **build** the
  `Content-Type` line.
- **`Encoding`.** Replaces PHPMailer's `8bit` default (and an empty value) with
  **`quoted-printable`**, so `★` = `E2 98 85` travels as `=E2=98=85` and survives a
  relay that does not advertise 8BITMIME. Chosen over base64 because the ASCII body
  stays readable in a raw source view — which is exactly what Gimli is reading.
  ⛔ An encoding somebody else set deliberately (e.g. `base64`) is left alone.
- **Off switch:** `bhp_email_phpmailer_charset_enabled`.
- **`bhp_email_html_content_type_header()`** — one place for
  `Content-Type: text/html; charset=UTF-8`, now used at the review-ask CLI
  test-send call site (which already stated it inline; the literal is kept as a
  fallback so a partial deploy cannot fatal).

Both mechanisms are kept. **Neither is trusted alone.**

### 2a. Two limits, stated rather than absorbed

- **It is not scoped to the engine and visit emails.** At `phpmailer_init` the
  message is a mailer object with no reliable back-reference to the `WC_Email` that
  built it. Sniffing a subject line to decide a charset would be worse engineering
  than applying the site's own charset to the site's own mail. The site charset is
  UTF-8. ⚠ If Gandalf wants it narrowed, the filter is the seam.
- **Nothing here proves the DELIVERED header changed.** That is Gimli's raw-source
  read plus a fresh FluentSMTP log row. It is not a code claim and is not made here.

### 2b. The test the brief asked for, and the honest gate on it

New **§14** in `tests\test-cycle179-review-seq.php`:

- **14.1** `WC_Email::get_headers()` states the charset, and no charset was appended
  twice. Guarded on §13's email object; skips loudly rather than fataling the suite.
- **14.2** `bhp_email_html_content_type_header()` exists and is well-formed.
- **14.3** **the headers as they reach `wp_mail`** — hooks the `wp_mail` filter to
  capture, hooks `pre_wp_mail` to guarantee nothing leaves the process, and asserts
  the charset on what was captured.
  ⛔ **Gated on `wp_mail()` still being WordPress's own**, checked with
  `ReflectionFunction`. If FluentSMTP has replaced it, `pre_wp_mail` cannot be
  assumed to be honoured, and **a test must never send a real email to prove a
  header**. In that case the probe is skipped and the skip is **printed** — and that
  printed line is the most useful diagnostic in the file, because a replaced
  `wp_mail()` re-parsing the header string is the leading explanation for log ids 6
  and 7.
- **14.4** the `phpmailer_init` handler: hooked; sets CharSet over a wrong one;
  replaces `8bit` with quoted-printable; leaves a deliberate `base64` alone; honours
  its off switch.
- **14.5** seal 1007 on all three sets, each **still usable**, plus the rendered
  HTML carrying no standalone `Andrew` while the signature block is intact.

## 3. Item 3 — the stale assertion, and it was wrong twice

`tests\test-visit-completed-email.php`, §9. Superseded line preserved verbatim:

    bhp_vce_assert( 4 === count( bhp_visit_email_body( $visit_email ) ),
        'visit order => four paragraphs (Amazon ask removed, seal 977)', $failures );

- **Wrong #1 — it named the retired set.** `4` was the `adams-2026-08-28` paragraph
  count. Seal 994 retired that set at 1.19.369; every slug now resolves to the one
  generic day-0 set, of a different length. The number stopped describing anything.
- **Wrong #2 — that fixture cannot render a body at all.** Its order is
  `bhp_vce_order( BHP_VCE_ADAMS )`: a slug and nothing else. No line items means
  `{BookTitle(s)}` does not resolve, and `bhp_visit_email_merge_is_complete()` is a
  **hard stop** on that slot (*"If no title resolves, do not send"*). The correct
  answer was an **empty** body — so the assertion could not have been green at any
  number.

Now: the hard stop is asserted explicitly, **and** the real body is asserted against
a **resolvable** order built entirely in memory — school from `_bhp_school_visit_school`
(order meta, which `bhp_visit_email_merge_values()` reads first), title from one
`WC_Order_Item_Product` whose product id is taken from `bhp_book_registry()` **at
runtime** rather than hard-coded. Seven rendered paragraphs from a set of eight
({VisitLine} correctly dropped), opening on the greeting with the `there` fallback,
**ending on the reply route**, no raw `{Slot}`, no bare `Andrew`. ⛔ Nothing is
saved; no product is created; no WooCommerce record is touched. If the registry
yields no product id the block **prints a SKIP and records a failure** rather than
passing silently.

§4's day-0 count moves 9 → 8, with content assertions on the new last paragraph.

### 3a. An environment trap found and disarmed before it fired

§13.6 asserted `stripos( $bhp_rs_html, 'facebook.com' ) === false` against the
ambient render. Correct while no Facebook URL existed anywhere — and **wrong the
moment `bhp_social_links` was set on staging with two real URLs**, at which point
the suite goes red for the software working exactly as designed. It now asserts the
invariant that matters — *nothing is emitted when nothing is supplied* — against a
render with the links explicitly emptied through the public filter, and **prints**
what the live option holds instead of asserting it. ⚠ This was not in the brief; it
was found while reading §13 and would have produced a false failure on the next run.

