## 2. Implementation decisions

**Seal 1010 — where the second greeting came from.** Observed, not guessed:
`rs370-day0.html` lines 53–55 show `div.email-introduction` opening with the
template's own `Hi %s,` and the approved copy's `Hi {ParentFirstName},`
immediately after. Two authors, one greeting each. **The copy's line stands**;
`woocommerce/emails/customer-completed-order.php` and its `plain/` twin now
print their own greeting only when `$bhp_visit_body` is empty. The ordinary
completed-order email is byte-identical: the whitespace was checked tag by tag
against the "PHP eats exactly one newline after `?>`" note already in that file.

**The signature block was lifted, not copied.** It lived inline in
`woocommerce/emails/bhp-review-ask.php`. Copying it into a second template
would have created the drift on day one, so it is now
`bhp_review_ask_signature_html()` / `bhp_review_ask_signature_text()` in
`inc/review-ask-email.php`, and **both** templates call it. The `<hr>` is part
of the block, so the two emails cannot disagree about the rule either. Markup
and colour values are unchanged from 1.19.370/371 — nothing was restyled.

**⚠ THE BRIEF'S PREMISE ON THE H1 WAS WRONG AND THE CORRECTION IS RECORDED, NOT
ABSORBED.** The brief said the H1 string is empty everywhere. It is empty on
touch 1, touch 2 and web touch 1 (`rs370-touch1.html`, `rs-touch1.html`,
`rs-touch2.html`, `rs-web1.html`, all line 30) but **not on day 0**, which
renders *"The signed books went home today"* (`rs370-day0.html` line 30). All
five read at this desk 2026-09-05.

So "the same position as touch 1" was resolved as **touch 1 shows no heading,
therefore day 0 shows none**: `bhp_visit_email_suppress_heading()` returns ''
from `woocommerce_email_heading_customer_completed_order` on the visit fork.
⛔ The copy set **keeps** its heading string, because
`bhp_visit_email_copy_is_usable()` requires a non-empty one and emptying it
there would have silently discarded the entire approved day-0 set. Nothing is
lost: the same sentence is still the subject and the preheader.

**The empty band itself.** The empty `<h1>` is `margin: 0` with no content, so
it is **zero pixels tall** — removing only the element would have looked like
compliance and changed nothing. What is visible is the wrapper cell's 20px of
top padding. `bhp_email_strip_empty_heading()` on `woocommerce_mail_content`
removes the empty `<h1>` **and** zeroes `#header_wrapper`'s padding. It is a
filter, not a template override, because overriding `emails/email-header.php`
is prohibited in this theme. It cannot touch an email that has a heading: the
pattern requires the `<h1>` to contain nothing but whitespace, and there is a
test asserting byte-for-byte return in that case.

**Stars — and the reason the previous hover may never have worked.** Observed
in `rs370-touch1.html`: Emogrifier had inlined the resting colour onto the
anchor (`<a ... style="...; color: #c9c2b3; ...">`) and the glyph sits in a
`<span aria-hidden="true">`. A `<style>` rule of `a:hover { color: … }` **loses
to that inline declaration**. The span carries no inline colour and an
`!important` author declaration outranks a normal inline one, so the rules are
now `a:hover span[aria-hidden] { color: … !important }` plus the same form of
the `:has()` fill-left rule — Legolas's prescription, adopted as written.
Resting colour `#c9c2b3` → `#dfc793`; hover unchanged at `#c4a15c`.
⚠ `#dfc793`'s contrast was **not** re-measured; it is stated as lower than the
2.2:1 Legolas measured for `#c4a15c`, by inspection of the hex. The caption
line and the per-star `aria-label` remain the load-bearing accessibility path.

**Hero — and a naming collision that will bite somebody.** Legolas's **output**
numbering is independent of the **gallery** numbering. Two different images are
called 05:

| File | What it is | Status |
|---|---|---|
| `read-aloud-dallas-harris-2026-09-03-05.jpg` | GALLERY frame 05 — second adult, no consent record, legible visitor badge | ⛔ **Barred.** `CYCLE179-DES-29(b)` open. Not shipped, not cropped, not referenced |
| `hero-dallas-harris-2026-09-03-05.jpg` | A **different** image, cropped from gallery frame **03**, no child in frame | Shipped, **not mapped** |

The 1.19.371 test asserted that no file ending `-05.jpg` existed in the theme.
That assertion was correct under the old numbering and **wrong under the new
one**, so it was rewritten to guard the gallery filename, with the reason
written above it in the test so it is not "fixed" back.

Touch 1 for `dallas-harris-2026-09-03` now maps to frame **04** — Legolas's own
first choice for a review ask. Day 0 keeps frame 01: seal 1015 was about the
review-ask picture and nothing was said about the day-0 one. All six new files
carry alt text, and there is now a test asserting every shipped hero has some.

**375px tables — the downloads table is NOT hidden, and here is why.** Read out
of `render-370-day0-375.png` at this desk: the downloads table carries two real,
useful things — the Adventure Activity Book PDF and the printable Vocabulary
Card Activity, each a live link that appears nowhere else in the message.
Hiding it would delete them. The observed failures were both the same cause:
**right-aligned cells in columns too narrow to hold them** — the download links
ladder over three ragged lines, and the 40-word hand-delivery description in the
totals block reads as a column of fragments. The fix is a `≤480px` block that
reduces cell padding and font, allows word-break, caps the line-item thumbnail,
and **left-aligns the right-aligned and `tfoot` cells**. The column headings are
**kept**: the usual `thead { display:none }` stack would leave "Never" with no
"Expires" above it, and this build has no way to re-label a stacked cell that
survives Gmail.

⚠ **Scope, stated plainly:** `.email-order-details` is the class WooCommerce puts
on the downloads table, the line-items table and the totals table, in **every**
transactional email in this store. This block therefore changes the ≤480px
rendering of all of them. That follows the round-10 ruling to keep the shared
mechanism global. It is presentation only — no cell, value or column order
changes — and Outlook desktop ignores `@media` entirely.

**Ruling from round 10 carried in:** seal 1007 on the legacy 21-day set —
⛔ **NOT DONE this round.** See §7.

