## 4. Item 3 — seal 1025, the day-0 shipping row

**Observed, in a render, not reasoned about.** `REVIEW-SEQ-STAGING\rs373-day0.html`
line 152, read byte-for-byte at this desk:

- `<th>`: `Hand delivery: Author hand-delivery at the Dallas Harris Elementary visit (September 3)`
- `<td>` (right-aligned, ~200px column): `Collection from <strong>Author hand-delivery at the Dallas Harris Elementary visit (September 3)</strong>:<br>Andrew brings the signed books to Dallas Harris Elementary on Thursday, September 3. Nothing is posted to your home, and there is no shipping charge. Visit time: 10:10 AM.`

The pickup name is printed **twice** and the sentence underneath is the
checkout's explanation, which the day-0 email's own approved body has already
given in full paragraphs above the table.

**What 1.19.374 renders instead** (transliterated into Python and run against
that exact cell — see §6):

- `<th>`: `Hand delivery:`
- `<td>`: `<div style="text-align:left;">Author hand-delivery at the Dallas Harris Elementary visit (September 3)</div>`

### ⛔ THE DEVIATION FROM THE BRIEF, STATED PLAINLY

**The brief offered two options:** the literal *"Hand delivered at the school
visit"* **or** *"the shortest truthful label the shipping method exposes"*.
**I took the second, deliberately.**

`bhp_school_pickup_label()` is marked in
`plugins\brave-hearts-bundle-pricing\includes\school-visit-pickup.php` as
*"APPROVED COPY, CARRIED FORWARD BYTE-IDENTICAL FROM 1.8.49. A label a parent
reads while paying is not something a mechanism change gets to reword on its own
initiative."* **Standing Rules §9: approved copy is locked; propose changes, do
not make them.** Replacing its rendering with a newly minted sentence in a
customer-facing email is exactly the act that rule forbids, and Gandalf's brief
is not the founder's approval for new copy.

⭐ **The first-choice literal is one filter callback away and the seam is
tested.** `apply_filters( 'bhp_visit_email_pickup_short_label', … )` — return
`'Hand delivered at the school visit'` and it renders, with no build. If Andrew
prefers it, say so and it is a one-line change.

### The scoping, and why there are two independent guards

1. **The template.** `bhp_visit_email_shorten_pickup_row()` is attached
   immediately before `woocommerce_email_order_details` and detached immediately
   after, and **only** when `$bhp_visit_body` is non-empty. An ordinary web
   receipt never has it attached at any moment, so **its order summary is
   byte-identical to 1.19.373**. Nothing is left hooked when the template
   returns.
2. **The function.** It rewrites nothing unless the row's own label starts with
   the plugin's shared phrase `Hand delivery`. A posted, charged order says
   `Shipping:` and falls straight through — which also means that with the
   bundle plugin inactive, the whole thing is a no-op.

⛔ **No plugin file and no WooCommerce setting is touched.** No shipping method,
zone, rate, title or pickup location is created or edited. No price, tax, total
or row order changes. This is a render-time override of one table cell in one
email, and the suite asserts every one of those negatives.

